NAME
    ls - list files and directories

SYNOPSIS
    ls [-1ACFUadchlp] [PATH...]

DESCRIPTION
    Lists each file operand or the immediate children of each directory in
    deterministic lexical order. By default, dot names are hidden and entries
    are placed in rows that fit COLUMNS, or 80 columns when COLUMNS is
    unavailable. PATH defaults to the current directory. Multiple directory
    operands receive headings.

OPTIONS
    -1  Print one entry per line.
    -C  Use terminal-width columns; this is the default unless -1 is used.
    -a  Include names beginning with a dot.
    -A  Include dot names except the special . and .. entries.
    -d  List directory operands themselves instead of their contents.
    -F  Append / to directories and @ to symbolic links. The filesystem ABI
        does not expose executable-mode or special-file classifiers.
    -p  Append / to directories.
    -l  Print one entry per line with a node-type marker, byte size, and a UTC
        time as YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM. The time shown is the modification time
        unless -c or -U selects another. The column appears only when at least
        one listed entry has the selected time: a provider that stores none,
        such as the read-only root or a quota-bound /tmp, reports absent and
        the column is omitted rather than left blank. TROE's filesystem ABI
        does not expose POSIX ownership or modes.

        There is no -u. No provider records an access time, so the option
        would have nothing to show; see ADR 0062.
    -c  With -l, show the change time instead of the modification time. It
        advances whenever an object's record is rewritten, including renames
        that leave the payload untouched. Only ext4 records one; FAT32 has no
        such field and reports none.
    -U  With -l, show the creation time instead of the modification time. It
        never advances once the object exists. ext4 and FAT32 both record one;
        the read-only root and /tmp do not.
    -h  With -l, round sizes using 1024-based IEC units such as KiB and GiB.

    Options may be combined. -- ends option parsing. Recursive listing and
    sorting by size or time are not provided because directory enumeration is
    intentionally bounded and returns one lexical page at a time.
